WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.853
Telling individual animals apart means comparing the markings in two photos.

2
00:00:04.853 --> 00:00:07.059
Image matchers already do this:

3
00:00:07.059 --> 00:00:11.030
they find keypoints and connect the ones that correspond.

4
00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:16.240
But these matchers were trained on buildings and streets. not on fur.

5
00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:20.090
and wildlife datasets have no keypoint labels to retrain them.

6
00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:24.797
What every monitoring project does have is identity labels:

7
00:00:24.797 --> 00:00:27.529
which photos show the same animal.

8
00:00:27.529 --> 00:00:29.350
WildMatch uses nothing else.

9
00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:33.978
The pretrained matcher scores each photo against the rest.

10
00:00:33.978 --> 00:00:38.005
Its highest-scoring photos of the same animal become positives.

11
00:00:38.005 --> 00:00:42.032
Its highest-scoring photos of other animals become hard negatives:

12
00:00:42.032 --> 00:00:44.270
the pairs it gets wrong.

13
00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:46.980
Training then asks for one thing:

14
00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:52.580
the score with a positive must beat the score with a hard negative by a margin.

15
00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:54.330
Only the matching module changes;

16
00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:57.130
the keypoints and descriptors stay as they were.

17
00:00:57.730 --> 00:01:02.199
After adaptation the matcher finds many more correspondences on the animal.

18
00:01:02.199 --> 00:01:07.480
and photos of the right individual fill the top of the candidate list.

19
00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:13.726
Across eight wildlife datasets. accuracy improves with about five GPU-hours of training.

20
00:01:13.726 --> 00:01:17.960
and every match can be inspected. line by line.

